architecture-decision-record:
  identifier: "syon_04"
  title: Independent Go implementation instead of FFI bindings
  status: accepted
  date: "2026-07-11"
  deciders:
    - felix
  superseded-by: ""
  context: |
    SYON has three language surfaces. Rust (crates/syon-parser,
    crates/syon-cli) is the primary implementation. Python
    (crates/syon-python) is a PyO3 extension module that binds directly
    to syon-parser -- it has no independent parsing logic; it converts
    the Rust value tree to Python objects. Go (syon-go) is a from-scratch,
    dependency-free, cgo-free parser and encoder. It does not call into
    the Rust crate at all; it has its own hand-written recursive-descent
    parser implementing the spec independently.

    Given syon-parser already exposes a C ABI (crates/syon-parser/src/ffi.rs,
    cdylib and staticlib outputs) for exactly this kind of cross-language
    reuse, Go bindings via cgo were a real alternative to writing a
    second parser from scratch.
  decision: |
    Give Go an independent, pure-Go implementation rather than cgo
    bindings to syon-parser.
  consequences:
    positive:
      - Go consumers get a dependency-free, cgo-free module -- no C toolchain, no cross-compilation pain from linking a Rust cdylib, no CGO_ENABLED gotchas. This matters more for a Go audience than for Python, where PyO3/maturin-built wheels already hide the FFI boundary from consumers.
    negative:
      - SYON's grammar and forbidden-construct rules are now implemented twice, independently, in Rust and in Go. They can drift apart.
      - There is no shared conformance test suite run against both implementations -- crates/syon-parser's tests and syon-go's tests are separate, and only agree by construction on the example fixtures each parses (see the examples-valid and go-build jobs in .github/workflows/ci.yml, and this ADR log itself, which both implementations also parse).
      - Any spec change needs to be ported to both implementations by hand; nothing enforces that they stay in sync beyond spec prose and code review.
  alternatives-rejected:
    -
      name: cgo bindings to syon-parser's existing C ABI
      reason: Would reuse one implementation, but forces every Go consumer to deal with a C toolchain and cross-compilation of a linked cdylib -- a worse default for the Go ecosystem than a dependency-free pure-Go module.
